        "title": "The Moai of Easter Island",
        "text": "Easter Island was named by a Dutchman, Jacob Roggeveen, who arrived there on Easter Day, 6 April 1722, but its native name is Rapa Nui, which is sometimes translated as 'centre of the Earth'. Nearly 4,025 km from the coast of Chile and 4,185 km from Tahiti, the island is a triangle of volcanic rock of just 17 square kilometres, and it is one of the most isolated places on Earth. The top of the highest of its three volcanoes, which are now extinct, is 511 m above sea level. Today the island has 3,000 inhabitants and a single town, Hanga Roa. Easter Island, or Rapa Nui, is now a nature reserve and is governed by Chile. The island is particularly famous for the large statues, called Moai, which are found there.\n\nIn 1989, the Chilean government invited Giuseppe Orifici, an Italian archaeologist, to visit the island. Impressed by the wealth of archaeological remains, Orifici arranged to start digging the following year. He co-ordinated a team of experts, each a specialist in their own field, who visited the island for several weeks at a time over the next ten years. These specialists ranged from archaeologists and anthropologists to botanists and experts in sculpture.\n\nScientists once believed that the Rapa Nui people had originally come from South America in about the 7th century. However, a few surviving traditions, as well as the shapes of some of the sculptures, show that the people came from Polynesia and probably arrived on the island in the 5th century. Recent research on bones and teeth strongly supports this theory. While the anthropologists Dr Andrea Drusini and Professor Daris Swindler were studying teeth from various sites on the island, they found that something known as a 'genetic bottleneck' had occurred. On a small island where people never marry outside their own clan, inbreeding is unavoidable, and as a result the gene pool of each group is narrow. A particular feature, such as large or missing teeth, then appears among family members much more regularly than would otherwise be expected, and this allowed the scientists to prove where the people had originally come from.\n\nThe bones were found mostly in burial chambers beneath the Moai. It is thought that a clan chief's social and economic power was measured by the size and number of Moai he had, so there was fierce competition between clan chiefs to build the biggest and best. Usually, each chief had between one and 15 Moai on his family's tomb, all of them between 3 m and 8 m tall. More were being built all the time: in total, nearly 12,000 are thought to have been made, using rock from the Rano Raraku volcano. The largest of the Moai found by Orifici's team was 33 m tall and weighed nearly 300 tonnes. Some of the sculptures were engraved – one has carvings of a boat – while others have large or small ears, depending on the clan that built them. Most have a pukao, a kind of hat made of red volcanic rock. Finished Moai were placed facing inwards towards the island, and they played an important part in the island's religion. The islanders worshipped them as ancestors who had become gods.\n\nThe building of Moai, and the religion connected with it, lasted until the 16th century. A number of factors led to its collapse, and to the near collapse of the Rapa Nui people, including population growth and the destruction of the forest. But it seems that the main reason was that the religion had simply grown too big for the island. More and more people were building ever larger Moai, and they were not spending enough time growing food or fishing. The fish close to the shore were hunted until they died out, and more and more trees were cut down to be used as rollers for moving the Moai. When there were no more trees, the land lost its fertility, people starved, and there was no wood to build boats to escape. Fortunately, a new, less demanding religion grew up during the 17th century, which saved the islanders and allowed the limited resources to be shared out more fairly.\n\nEuropeans arrived in the 18th century and destroyed much of what remained by bringing in foreign diseases and introducing a few species which destroyed the native plants and animals. It was also at this time that the key to the Rapa Nui written language was lost.\n\nThe history of Rapa Nui and its people should be a warning to all of us in the 21st century: we must take care of our natural resources before it is too late."
